  "response": "A contested attribution is settled not by choosing the more impressive method but by asking what each body of evidence is competent to establish. Stylometry analyses quantifiable textual features such as word and character frequencies. Function word frequencies serve as common authorial markers in stylometric analysis. Although the method rests on measurable and replicable quantities, small text samples reduce the reliability of stylometric attribution, so that the extent of the disputed text and of the comparison corpus is itself a matter for argument. By contrast, stylometric evidence is treated as conclusive proof of authorship under United States federal rules of evidence.\n\nThe documentary side of the inquiry proceeds along quite different lines. Documentary records used in attribution disputes include publishers' ledgers, contracts, and private correspondence. An investigator reads such materials for the administrative residue that the making of a book inevitably leaves behind: who was paid, who corrected proofs, who was told in advance what was coming. Because such papers were generated for other purposes, their value turns less on interpretation than on provenance and dating, and a well-documented chain of custody can outweigh a statistical profile drawn from a thin sample.\n\nDating is accordingly the first point at which a dispute can be closed. Primary Colors was published anonymously in 1996. It was first published anonymously in 1989, a dating that cannot be squared with its appearance in 1996, since both cannot be true of a single first edition, and one of them must be discarded before any argument about authorship can proceed.\n\nThe Primary Colors case shows how the two kinds of testimony converge on a single name. Donald Foster named Joe Klein as the author of Primary Colors; in other words, his analysis identified the anonymous author of that novel as Joe Klein. The novel's distribution of function words matched that of Joe Klein's signed journalism, which confirms that identification of the author. At least one of two supports for it holds, and perhaps both: the correspondence between the novel's function words and the signed journalism, or the presence of physical traces of Joe Klein's involvement in the publisher's manuscript files. Donald Foster attributed the 1612 poem \"A Funeral Elegy\" to William Shakespeare, an attribution made well before the rival case for a different dramatist was put into print. The Klein identification held, whereas the Elegy attribution ended quite otherwise. Donald Foster's withdrawal of the Shakespeare attribution of \"A Funeral Elegy\" in 2002 resulted from Gilles Monsarrat's argument that the poem had been written by John Ford.",
